/* PG-MLP scheduler of N-i switching coefficients (generated by deployment.py; do not edit).
 *
 * Contract
 *   pgmlp_coefficients(A, kappa_hat, K) writes K = {K12, K13, K23} for a move of |A| rad, Eqs. (4)-(12),
 *   and returns a status (bit flags, 0 = PGMLP_OK):
 *     PGMLP_OK          |A| in [PGMLP_A_MIN, PGMLP_A_MAX] rad and kappa_hat in [PGMLP_K_MIN, PGMLP_K_MAX]
 *                       (the training domain);
 *     PGMLP_A_BELOW     |A| < PGMLP_A_MIN: extrapolated prediction; in the tests (3e-4 ... 3e-3 rad) the settling
 *                       time degraded to tau1 <= 1.69, still far better than the analytic coefficients
 *                       (tau1 up to 51) - extend the training range if such moves are commanded;
 *     PGMLP_A_ABOVE     |A| > PGMLP_A_MAX: extrapolated prediction (tested up to 10 rad: tau1 <= 1.01, the
 *                       gain of 1 ... 1.5 % over the law comes only with an overshoot of about 0.5 %);
 *     PGMLP_K_OUTSIDE   kappa_hat outside [PGMLP_K_MIN, PGMLP_K_MAX]: extrapolated prediction (tested up to
 *                       2.32, which occurs with the inertia margin kappa_hat / 0.9);
 *     PGMLP_FALLBACK    input outside the tested range or invalid (non-finite, kappa_hat outside
 *                       [PGMLP_K_HARD_MIN, PGMLP_K_HARD_MAX], |A| outside [PGMLP_A_HARD_MIN, PGMLP_A_HARD_MAX])
 *                       or a non-finite intermediate result: the analytic coefficients (5), (6) are
 *                       returned (for kappa_hat = 1 if kappa_hat itself is invalid). The hard limits are
 *                       the range covered by the tests; widen them only after testing.
 *   The sign of A is ignored (the drive is symmetric). Guarantees for every input: K12, K13, K23 finite and
 *   positive, and K13 <= K12^2 / 4, i.e. real negative roots of the outer sliding equation (Eq. (12)).
 *   Single-precision arithmetic only (float, logf, log10f, expf, tanhf); no dynamic memory; C99. */
